1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:01,960
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02,310 --> 00:00:05,195
The store, the tools
and the policy are ready.

3
00:00:05,545 --> 00:00:11,190
Now we connect them in the agent loop,
run a full demo, break things on purpose, and

4
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:12,400
look at the tests.

5
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:18,321
Then we'll wrap up the module with
interview questions, a quiz, and your extension

6
00:00:18,321 --> 00:00:19,001
challenge.

7
00:00:20,801 --> 00:00:22,428
First, the system prompt.

8
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:27,550
Notice the style from Module
3: it explains why, not just what.

9
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:33,727
The tools are the only way to read or
change the data, so the model has a reason to

10
00:00:33,727 --> 00:00:34,359
use them.

11
00:00:34,959 --> 00:00:40,276
It asks for parallel tool calls when
the user wants several independent things.

12
00:00:40,876 --> 00:00:46,636
And it says how to behave: look up a
task's number first, fix the input after an

13
00:00:46,636 --> 00:00:50,308
error, and never retry
an action the user declined.

14
00:00:50,658 --> 00:00:55,123
Today's date is filled in, so the
model can write due dates correctly.

15
00:00:56,923 --> 00:01:00,854
Act runs one tool call
through run tool, with the policy.

16
00:01:01,204 --> 00:01:07,452
Any tool error, invalid input, a denied
action, a timeout, becomes a step marked as

17
00:01:07,452 --> 00:01:08,129
an error.

18
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:09,658
Never a crash.

19
00:01:10,258 --> 00:01:16,292
Run is the loop you know from Module
4: a step limit, a budget check, and clean

20
00:01:16,292 --> 00:01:19,271
stops for a refusal or a cut-off reply.

21
00:01:19,871 --> 00:01:22,944
No tool calls means
the model has answered.

22
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:24,842
And here's what's new.

23
00:01:25,192 --> 00:01:31,171
We act on every tool call in the reply,
in order, report each step to the trace, and

24
00:01:31,171 --> 00:01:32,594
collect the results.

25
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:38,615
Then all the results go back in one
single user message, exactly as lesson two

26
00:01:38,615 --> 00:01:39,124
showed.

27
00:01:40,924 --> 00:01:43,415
Now a full demo, from an empty store.

28
00:01:44,015 --> 00:01:45,144
First, reset.

29
00:01:45,744 --> 00:01:47,691
Three tasks in one request.

30
00:01:48,041 --> 00:01:52,499
One turn with three parallel
calls, and each task gets its number.

31
00:01:53,099 --> 00:01:56,848
Complete task three, and
save a note, again in one turn.

32
00:01:57,448 --> 00:02:03,343
And two read tools together: all tasks,
with buy milk marked done, and the note we

33
00:02:03,343 --> 00:02:04,134
just saved.

34
00:02:05,934 --> 00:02:08,337
Now the safety features, on purpose.

35
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:13,114
Delete task one: the agent asks,
with the task's title and due date.

36
00:02:13,464 --> 00:02:16,045
The answer is no, so pay rent stays.

37
00:02:16,645 --> 00:02:21,187
Delete task two, and this time
the answer is yes, so it's deleted.

38
00:02:21,787 --> 00:02:24,409
In read-only mode, writes are blocked.

39
00:02:25,009 --> 00:02:30,217
And a rejected API key ends with
one clear line, and exit code one.

40
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:32,163
No traceback anywhere.

41
00:02:33,963 --> 00:02:39,839
The tests run the real loop, the real
tools and the real policy, against the fake

42
00:02:39,839 --> 00:02:42,305
model, with a temporary data file.

43
00:02:42,655 --> 00:02:48,289
This one checks parallel calls: three
steps, all in turn one, and the results

44
00:02:48,289 --> 00:02:51,435
matched to the calls
by ID, in one message.

45
00:02:52,035 --> 00:02:58,546
This one checks recovery from invalid
input: first an error step, then a successful

46
00:02:58,546 --> 00:03:01,056
one, and exactly one task saved.

47
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:07,431
And these check the human approval: when
the answer is no, the task is still there,

48
00:03:07,431 --> 00:03:09,588
and the policy logged declined.

49
00:03:09,938 --> 00:03:11,978
When it's yes, the task is gone.

50
00:03:13,778 --> 00:03:19,734
Forty-three tests, all passing, with no
key, no network, and without touching your

51
00:03:19,734 --> 00:03:20,823
real data file.

52
00:03:21,423 --> 00:03:24,004
And mypy is happy with every type hint.

53
00:03:25,804 --> 00:03:31,739
A four hundred about the tool schema
usually means a strict schema rule: additional

54
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:35,387
properties must be
false, and every field required.

55
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:39,497
Generate schemas with
schema for, instead of by hand.

56
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:44,598
A four hundred about tool use
IDs means a result is missing.

57
00:03:44,948 --> 00:03:48,972
Every call needs its result,
all in the next user message.

58
00:03:49,572 --> 00:03:55,081
If the model picks the wrong tool,
improve the descriptions, and remove tools that

59
00:03:55,081 --> 00:03:55,619
overlap.

60
00:03:56,219 --> 00:04:00,348
And if the agent seems stuck,
a tool probably has no timeout.

61
00:04:00,698 --> 00:04:02,075
Every tool gets one.

62
00:04:03,875 --> 00:04:05,627
Module interview corner.

63
00:04:05,977 --> 00:04:10,354
Question one: walk me through what
happens when an agent calls a tool.

64
00:04:12,954 --> 00:04:15,754
Your code sends the
tools and the messages.

65
00:04:16,104 --> 00:04:21,065
The model replies with a stop
reason of tool use, and tool use blocks.

66
00:04:21,415 --> 00:04:27,415
Your code validates each input, checks
the permission, runs it with a timeout, and

67
00:04:27,415 --> 00:04:33,342
returns the tool results, with the same
IDs, and is error on failures, all in one

68
00:04:33,342 --> 00:04:34,293
user message.

69
00:04:34,643 --> 00:04:37,415
Then repeat until there are no tool calls.

70
00:04:37,765 --> 00:04:43,496
The trap: skipping validation and
permissions, or saying the API runs the tool.

71
00:04:44,096 --> 00:04:49,374
Question two, a scenario: how would
you let an agent send emails safely?

72
00:04:51,974 --> 00:04:54,065
Make send email a danger tool.

73
00:04:54,415 --> 00:04:56,937
Validate the recipients and the content.

74
00:04:57,287 --> 00:05:01,802
Show the human the exact email,
and ask for approval every time.

75
00:05:02,152 --> 00:05:08,159
Log every decision, limit how many it
can send, and start with a draft-only mode.

76
00:05:08,509 --> 00:05:12,024
The trap: adding never
send spam to the system prompt.

77
00:05:12,374 --> 00:05:14,356
Instructions are not enforcement.

78
00:05:14,956 --> 00:05:20,222
And question three: what's the
difference between strict tool use and validating in

79
00:05:20,222 --> 00:05:20,856
your code?

80
00:05:23,456 --> 00:05:28,996
Strict tool use guarantees the input
matches the schema: the fields and the types.

81
00:05:29,346 --> 00:05:35,242
Your validation enforces limits and
business rules, and turns failures into clear

82
00:05:35,242 --> 00:05:37,061
errors the model can fix.

83
00:05:37,411 --> 00:05:39,897
The trap: treating them as alternatives.

84
00:05:40,247 --> 00:05:41,344
You want both.

85
00:05:43,144 --> 00:05:44,450
Module recap.

86
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:50,433
With tools, the model requests, your
code runs, and the results come back by ID.

87
00:05:51,033 --> 00:05:55,610
A registry and Pydantic give
one source of truth for every tool.

88
00:05:56,210 --> 00:06:00,918
For parallel calls, run them
all, and send one message of results.

89
00:06:01,518 --> 00:06:05,210
Validate every input, and
give every tool a timeout.

90
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:11,297
And use least privilege, permission
levels, and a human yes for anything dangerous.

91
00:06:13,097 --> 00:06:14,993
Module quiz, question one.

92
00:06:15,343 --> 00:06:17,576
Who actually runs a tool's function?

93
00:06:23,776 --> 00:06:26,201
Your code, after the model requests it.

94
00:06:26,551 --> 00:06:28,111
The model only asks.

95
00:06:29,911 --> 00:06:31,001
Question two.

96
00:06:31,351 --> 00:06:34,773
Why generate tool
schemas from Pydantic classes?

97
00:06:40,973 --> 00:06:46,313
Because one class both describes the
input to the model, and validates it in your

98
00:06:46,313 --> 00:06:46,642
code.

99
00:06:46,992 --> 00:06:48,368
One source of truth.

100
00:06:50,168 --> 00:06:51,299
Question three.

101
00:06:51,649 --> 00:06:54,863
With strict mode on,
which check is still your job?

102
00:07:01,063 --> 00:07:03,709
Checking that the
task with that ID exists.

103
00:07:04,059 --> 00:07:05,925
Strict mode covers the shape.

104
00:07:06,275 --> 00:07:08,005
Business rules are yours.

105
00:07:09,805 --> 00:07:10,919
Question four.

106
00:07:11,269 --> 00:07:14,090
A thread timeout fires after two seconds.

107
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:15,832
What does it guarantee?

108
00:07:22,032 --> 00:07:25,640
That the agent stops
waiting, and gets an error result.

109
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:27,905
It doesn't stop the work itself.

110
00:07:29,705 --> 00:07:31,069
And question five.

111
00:07:31,419 --> 00:07:34,822
Where must the human
approval check for delete task live?

112
00:07:41,022 --> 00:07:44,277
In your code, between
validation and execution.

113
00:07:44,627 --> 00:07:46,133
Prompts guide the model.

114
00:07:46,483 --> 00:07:48,059
Only code enforces.

115
00:07:49,859 --> 00:07:52,267
Now some practice, from easy to hard.

116
00:07:52,617 --> 00:07:53,963
Pause and try them.

117
00:07:54,313 --> 00:07:57,875
The answers are in the
module's quiz dot md file.

118
00:07:58,475 --> 00:07:59,114
One.

119
00:07:59,464 --> 00:08:05,142
Write a Pydantic class for a rename
task tool, with a task ID and a new title.

120
00:08:05,742 --> 00:08:06,364
Two.

121
00:08:06,714 --> 00:08:11,819
Which permission level would you give a
tool that clears all completed tasks, and

122
00:08:11,819 --> 00:08:12,071
why?

123
00:08:12,671 --> 00:08:13,368
Three.

124
00:08:13,718 --> 00:08:17,146
The model sends four
tool calls, and one times out.

125
00:08:17,496 --> 00:08:19,757
What does the next user message contain?

126
00:08:20,357 --> 00:08:22,009
And four, a scenario.

127
00:08:22,359 --> 00:08:25,301
A customer support
agent can issue refunds.

128
00:08:25,651 --> 00:08:28,260
Design its tools, and its safety checks.

129
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:37,667
Your extension challenge.

130
00:08:38,017 --> 00:08:42,698
Pick one or more, and write a
test for each, using the fake API.

131
00:08:43,298 --> 00:08:46,078
Add a rename task tool, as a write tool.

132
00:08:46,678 --> 00:08:51,057
Add a due soon tool, that
lists open tasks due within N days.

133
00:08:51,657 --> 00:08:55,639
Log every policy decision to
an audit file, with the time.

134
00:08:56,239 --> 00:09:01,477
Or run search notes in a separate
process, so a timeout really stops the work.

135
00:09:03,277 --> 00:09:07,349
These are the official
documentation pages for this module.

136
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:12,517
Links are in the description, along
with the full source code, and the course

137
00:09:12,517 --> 00:09:13,080
glossary.

138
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:20,418
That's Module 5 complete.

139
00:09:20,768 --> 00:09:26,810
Your agent can now use many tools,
check every input, survive slow tools, and ask

140
00:09:26,810 --> 00:09:29,346
before it does anything dangerous.

141
00:09:29,696 --> 00:09:35,214
In Module 6, we give agents memory:
what to remember, what to forget, and how to

142
00:09:35,214 --> 00:09:37,697
pick up a conversation the next day.

143
00:09:38,047 --> 00:09:38,989
See you there.
